{"id":"h181","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-physics","question":"What voltage will send a current of 2 amperes through a bell circuit if the resistance of the circuit is 3 ohms?\nIs the correct answer \"9 volts\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"Ohm's law gives V = I × R.","confidence":0.98,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.5394,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"With 2 A and 3 Ω, the voltage is 6 volts.","confidence":0.98,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5394,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"9 volts is not the correct answer.","confidence":0.97,"lean":-1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Getting 9 would require adding current and resistance, which gives 5 rather than 9.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-0.5,"theta":1.2566,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Getting 9 volts would require misapplying a formula, such as multiplying by an extra factor.","confidence":0.7,"lean":-0.6,"theta":1.0996,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"No consideration in favor of 9 volts holds up.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Ohm's law governs a simple resistive circuit with steady current.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.4,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The calculation V = IR is a direct, unambiguous application to the given values and decides the matter.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.9,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":3,"J":0.2,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":4,"J":0.2,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":6,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":7,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":3,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":4,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":5,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":7,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":3,"j":4,"J":0.5,"relation"